Thornswood

Copyright (c) 2026 Patrick (GitHub: EvilPatrick06), the owner of Thornswood.
All rights reserved.

This repository holds Thornswood's own work: the launcher, the installer and
its setup program, the game plugins and overlays, the gamemode and its data,
the content plugin, the website, the Discord bot, the voice service, the
scripts, the tests, the documents and the lore. No licence to use, copy,
change or share any of it is granted, except as this file says.

THE TEAM

A collaborator on this repository may read, run, change and build it, only to
work on Thornswood. That permission ends when their access to the repository
ends.

WHAT REACHES THE PUBLIC

Thornswood hands people a launcher, an installer, game plugins and overlays
in readable source, the website's pages and scripts, and a content plugin on
Nexus Mods. Each is given so people can play on Thornswood's servers and for
nothing else. Being able to read a file is not a licence to copy, change or
share it.

OTHER PEOPLE'S WORK

Some files in this repository, and in what Thornswood ships, belong to other
people and stay under their own licences. THIRD-PARTY.md lists each one with
its licence and where its source is. This file changes none of those
licences, and it does not cover the SkyMP fork in engine/skymp, which keeps
its own.

CONTRIBUTOR TERMS

Everybody who contributes to this repository (code, text, art, data or any
other change, by commit, pull request or any other way) agrees to these
terms once, in #staff-general, before their first contribution. Agreeing
covers what they contributed before that as well.

1. You keep the copyright in what you contribute.
2. You give the owner of Thornswood a permanent, worldwide, free,
   non-exclusive and irrevocable licence to use, copy, change, publish,
   distribute and sublicense what you contribute, as part of Thornswood or
   on its own, and to put it under any other licence later, an open one
   included.
3. You have the right to give that licence: the work is your own, or its
   owner has given you leave, and the pull request says whose it is.
4. You give it as it is, with no warranty of any kind.
